    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# -------------------------------------
    16	# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
    17	#    Original abc82100 used a global color dictionary. A compact legend
    18	#    `src,tgt,anchor+teal` taught "every other pixel of color src becomes this
    19	#    teal stencil recolored to tgt".
    20	#    This combo changes two linked stages at once:
    21	#    - D16/0: the legend color pair is no longer adjacent. It is a cardinal
    22	#      ray `S . . T A` or `S . . . T A`, so source and target are separated by
    23	#      a fixed span of 3 or 4 cells for the whole grid.
    24	#    - D10/2: each legend only applies to one nearby execution source of the
    25	#      same color, placed at Chebyshev distance 2 or 3 from the legend source.
    26	#    Small example:
    27	#      input cluster: legend source S at (5,5), target T at (5,8), anchor A at
    28	#      (5,9), teal stencil touching A, and a second S at (7,4).
    29	#      output cluster: erase the legend and teal cells, then translate the teal
    30	#      stencil so that A lands on (7,4) and recolor it to T's color.
    31	#    Difference from the original:
    32	#    - the solver must first detect the non-adjacent source-target relation;
    33	#    - then it must pair each legend with a local execution source instead of
    34	#      applying the rule to every pixel of that source color globally.
    35	# b) Complexity calibration:
    36	#    - One span is shared by the whole grid and chosen from {3, 4}. This keeps
    37	#      the long-distance cue obvious without exploding the search space.
    38	#    - Each grid has 2-5 local clusters, and at least one source color is reused
    39	#      across multiple clusters with different targets. This blocks a degenerate
    40	#      global-color interpretation.
    41	#    - Bystanders use colors outside all legend source/target colors so they do
    42	#      not become accidental execution candidates.
    43	# c) Implicit-assumption review:
    44	#    - Original `_discover_rules` assumed `target` was a 4-neighbor; here it
    45	#      must scan a small set of longer spans and select the dominant span.
    46	#    - Original `main()` assumed source color uniquely identified a rule; that
    47	#      is false here because the same source color intentionally appears in
    48	#      multiple nearby clusters with different targets.
    49	#    - Original `main()` deleted every non-legend pixel whose color matched a
    50	#      learned source color; that would wrongly erase execution pixels belonging
    51	#      to other local legends. The combo solver therefore erases only execution
    52	#      pixels that were explicitly assigned to a nearby legend.
    53	#    - The new assignment rule assumes each execution source is the unique
    54	#      nearest same-colored candidate within distance {2,3} of its own legend.
    55	#      The generator enforces this by spacing footprints apart and checking
    56	#      same-color clusters against each other.
    57	# d) Placement legality:
    58	#    - Every legend uses `target = source + span*dir` and `anchor = target + dir`
    59	#      with all cells in bounds.
    60	#    - The execution source is never on the legend ray, stays at Chebyshev
    61	#      distance 2 or 3 from the legend source, and stays at least 2 cells away
    62	#      from the legend pair and teal stencil.
    63	#    - Future colored placements avoid reserved cardinal rays around all colored
    64	#      cells, preventing accidental extra long-distance legends.
    65	# e) Visual observability:
